                              FLYING ANOTHER PLANE TYPE

           You'll notice that the plane type of the first F-16 is no
           longer limited in the "Select Aircraft Type" dialog. Simply
           change the plane as desired.  As usual, if the type you want
           isn't listed, use the "Set Aircraft..." button to set the
           desired types.

           After you do this, StratFalc does everything for you--
           automatically.

           To accommodate the increased weapon loads available with the
           new aircraft, the executable file (FALCON3.EXE/FALCONCD.EXE)
           had to be modified.  When you save your mission, StratFalc
           checks to see if *you* are flying an F-16.  If you're not, it
           makes changes to the Falcon executable.  These changes prevent
           Falcon itself from removing the weapons you've placed on the
           plane.

           WHEN YOU ENTER THE "LOAD ARMAMENT" SCREEN IN FALCON, IT WILL
           APPEAR AS IF THERE ARE NO WEAPONS ON THE AIRCRAFT. THIS SCREEN
           IS IN ERROR--THE PLANE IS LOADED JUST AS YOU SET IT IN
           STRATEGIC FALCON.  The only buttons you should hit in this
           screen are "TAKEOFF" to get on with the mission or "ASSIGN
           PILOTS" to return to the previous screen.  Do NOT try to load
           weapons here.  Just to remind you of this, the screen name (as
           well as the label of the button that you used to get here) has
           been changed to "STRTFALC LOAD." This means that StratFalc has
           taken over control of the loadouts--do not attempt to adjust
           them from this screen.

           If the plane in the first slot *is* an F-16, StratFalc will
           patch the Falcon executable back to normal.

           Also, once you're flying the plane, there's a cockpit cue to
           remind you what type you're flying (as if the flight model
           isn't enough).  Where you normally see the letters "M61" to the
           left of the radar screen (if the cannon is currently selected),
           you'll see an indicator of the aircraft type. For instance, "F-
           4" in the block indicates--surprise!-- you're flying an F-4.
           It gets a little tougher for F-117s and F-111s, since there are
           only 3 letters to work with. Those are "117" and "111"
           respectively.  (Note:  this designation will be accurate only
           if you've just come from StratFalc; it will updated for the new
           aircraft type the next time you run Strategic Falcon.)

           Please note the ability to properly load weapons on the new
           planes is supported ONLY in Falcon 3.04 and 3.05.  Earlier
           versions of Falcon are not supported.





           Strategic FALCON        Version 1.2 Update          Page 3
           ------------------------------------------------------------

           A new caution is necessary:

           Several types of Falcon 3.0 missions require that an *F-16*
           survives.  Now that you can fly any type of aircraft, many
           times no F-16 will even be assigned, so it becomes impossible
           to successfully complete these missions.  In general, it's okay
           to not assign an F-16 in any type of air- to-ground mission;
           it's the air-to-air type missions that are troublesome.  BUT!
           Strategic Falcon is smart enough to know what type of mission
           is being assigned and is also smart enough to know when there
           is no F-16 present, so it will warn you if you're about to
           start one of the "trouble" missions without a Viper in hand.
           Specifically, you'll see this warning when you try to leave
           StratFalc:

                    This mission requires the survival of 1 F-16.
                    None are assigned--mission will fail!  Exit?

           If you answer "Yes," you're on your own.  You should answer
           "No" and assign an F-16, even if you just have it do nothing
           but land and wait for you. (A complete list of the specific
           mission types that require F-16s is given later.)

                                  REALISTIC COLUMNS

           This option is enabled by going to the Other/Config submode and
           pressing the "More..." button.  Check the box that says
           "Realistic Columns (Scatter)." Click on "OK"; click "Save Cfg"
           if you want the change to be permanent.

           Whenever you fly a ground strike mission in Falcon from now on
           (assuming you've used StratFalc for that mission), you'll see
           that both friendly and enemy ground units no longer line up in
           such a perfect "kill us all at once" style.  One of the more
           intriguing aspects is that cluster bombs will still work to
           wipe out the softer targets, but they'll have to be placed with
           greater precision to maximize the effect of their blast.  (Plus
           the scattered columns just look better!)


                                THE AIRBORNE BRIGADE

           Several users requested this feature.  In "Sim Mode," your 3rd
           Brigade can now be dropped anywhere in the theater, but YOU CAN
           DEPLOY IT ONLY ONCE PER CAMPAIGN.  Just press the new "Deploy
           Airborne" button in the Strat/Sim/Grnd Submode.  You'll see
           that the cursor changes to a gunsight and jumps to the center
           of the map, and the map itself goes to a zero, zero origin with
           a 1X scale.





           Strategic FALCON        Version 1.2 Update          Page 4
           ------------------------------------------------------------

           Place the gunsight on the spot where you wish to drop your
           troops and push the left mouse button.  A confirmation box will
           ask if you're sure you want to do this.

           Once the brigade has been deployed, the "Deploy Airborne"
           button will remain depressed for the remainder of the campaign,
           indicating to you that you've already used this option.

           Bear in mind that you can still use the 3rd Brigade as you
           always have--they can be moved just like either of the other
           brigades.


                       COMPATIBILITY WITH "FALCON TEST PILOT"

           First, what is Falcon Test Pilot?

           Basically, it is a utility that allows you to modify any of the
           flight models and aircraft attributes in Falcon.  You can make
           changes ranging from the size of a plane's crew to its
           resistance to radar lock to how much damage it can take before
           it goes down.  FTP's forte, though, is in allowing you to
           change the actual flight models.

           Falcon Test Pilot allows you to fly any of the various flight
           models in Falcon, as well as the ones you modify (hence the
           name!).  Also, it has features to enhance head- to-head play.

           StratFalc supports the enhanced flight models that FTP allows
           you to create. With FTP and SF together you will now
           essentially have more than one version of an aircraft type at
           your disposal for any campaign mission.

           For instance, right now with StratFalc, you have (and can fly)
           the normal F-4.  With Falcon Test Pilot, you could

           specify the F-4G Wild Weasel aircraft with enhanced avionics
           and ECM capability (which you'll create yourself in FTP).

           Also, for some of the aircraft in Falcon (namely the
           helicopters), FTP is required to make them at all flyable. Some
           others, like the B-52, have disappointing flight models.

