           Here is a list of the types of missions which require F16s (and
           that F-16 must survive!):

                     Scramble
                     Air Superiority
                     Front Line Screen
                     Combat Air Patrol
                     Escort Transport
                     Escort Strike Aircraft
                     Escort Bombers
                     Recon Ships
                     Rendezvous Escort
                     Havcap
                     Supply Cap

           These types of missions do NOT require placement of an F-16:

                     Close Air Support
                     Ground Strike
                     Bomb
                     Beach Assault
                     Defend Beaches
                     Attack Ships
                     SEAD Antiship
                     SEAD
                     Airborne Assault Interdiction
                     Force Protection
                     Ground Transport Interdiction

           StratFalc will warn you if you don't have an F-16 assigned when
           you need one.  You could look on this requirement as giving
           Falcon a whole new level.  If you only have a lone F- 16
           assigned to fulfill this requirement, he's like your king in a
           game of chess:  protect him at all costs.

           Here's a little more info about weapons loads.  If you're not
           flying an F-16, the Load Armament ("StrtFalc Load") screen will
           show a clean F-16 (as mentioned above), even though the
           aircraft is correctly loaded.  You're advised not to press any
           buttons *other* than "Takeoff" and "Assign Pilots."  You should
           be aware that all the other buttons *work*;  that is, if you





           Strategic FALCON        Version 1.2 Update          Page 7
           ------------------------------------------------------------

           press "Load" on the AIM-9Ps, you will be adding two of those
           missiles to the weapon load already on the aircraft, although
           you will have no visual cue that this has occurred (other than
           the available quantity of the missile being decreased).  You
           will overload your plane in short order if you keep this up!

           The first few times you see the empty F-16 you will probably
           instantly react by trying to add weapons;  when you don't see
           them go onto the wings, you'll add more.  If you've hopelessly
           overloaded the plane you have two choices:  exit Falcon and go
           back to StratFalc and fix the mess (don't ignore the warning
           next time!), or hit "Default Load." This button still works as
           well;  although you still can't see them, you'll have a normal
           F-16 load on your wings.

           Another thing about flying different planes concerns your
           wingmen.  If you're flying an aircraft of a different type than
           your wingman, make sure he can keep up with you.  This is
           especially important if you're flying out of a base that's at
           the bottom of a valley (Bosnia is full of them).  Your wingmen
           will very easily crash into the cliffs.  If they seem to
           mysteriously vanish, check this first:  put your plane in
           autopilot and jump to wingman view.  Watch him play chicken
           with the side of the mountain and lose.

           There are a few ways out of this.  It helps to bear in mind
           that your wingman's flying skill and general brainpower can be
           pretty low.  In his mind, he's got this wonderful little
           racetrack pattern that he wants to fly until you get up in the
           air, and unless you do something that gets him out of that
           racetrack, he's not going to realize that he's headed for a
           sudden stop until it's too late.  By the time "AVOID ROCK FACE"
           makes it into his scheme of things, he probably won't have the
           smash to accomplish it.

           The easiest way to get him out of racetrack mode is to tell him
           to break left or right (as applicable) as soon as you get into
           the cockpit.  Breaking high is not a good idea because he'll be
           moving slow and might stall, and breaking low is right out.
           Something else you might try is telling him to close up the
           formation (<SHIFT-F>), or open up (<SHIFT-G).  Or you can try
           breaking sharply yourself--away from any mountains--as soon as
           you're off the runway.  He may counter by turning sharply to
           get in formation.

           Also, be aware that even after he falls in on your wing, he
           climb out as well as you.  It is often worthwhile to do a slow
           spiral up from the runway to a few thousand feet--with him in
           tow--before you head over any mountains.





