           STRAT.BAT
           SF.EXE
           This is the core program.  It's the greatest development in
           human civilization since Eve.



                               COMMS--SUCH AS THEY ARE

           Okay, first off, this whole thing is squirrely, but here's what
           I've come up with.  In a radical departure from my earlier
           philosophy, I decided to hack the Falcon executable, which is
           dangerous to say the least.  My goal was to allow free
           modification of the SQUAD6.MIS file, so its hidden/read-only





           Strategic FALCON        Version 1.2 Update          Page 14
           ------------------------------------------------------------

           bit could be set and Falcon would use that file in comms mode,
           without causing a "Cannot Create File" fatal error.

           The new modification supports Falcon 3.04 and 3.05 only.  In my
           experience, 3.04 is more stable than 3.05.  In any case, we're
           in essentially uncharted waters.  Remember, Falcon was *not*
           written with this stuff in mind!

           Before I could proceed, the existing Falcon 3.05 "Cannot Create
           File-SQUAD6.MTV" problem for campaign comms had to be fixed.  I
           did that by changing two bytes in the exe, and the result of
           that fix is the file 305FIX.EXE.  So the first step in getting
           StratFalc support for comms is:

           I.  If you're using Falcon version 3.05, run 305FIX.EXE on all
           machines on the network (or both machines on the null- modem
           cable).

           You only have to do this once.  Even without StratFalc support,
           at least you'll be able to play campaign comms in 3.05 now.
           Make sure you clear any old (hidden) SQUAD6.MTVs off all
           machines before proceeding.

           Next, the Cannot Create File-SQUAD6.MIS problem was tackled.
           The result of this hack was COMMHACK.EXE.  It can be run two
           ways--COMMHACK ALTER will change Falcon so that the error
           message will be bypassed, and COMMHACK RESTORE will put it back
           to normal.  All machines must support this hack, so the second
           step is:

           II.  Run COMMHACK ALTER on all machines on the network (or both
           machines on the null-modem cable).

           Okay, now we've got all the Falcon exes where we want them.
           Unfortunately, the way Falcon's comms work, we need to do more
           to make this work.

           First you have to understand:  what we do depends on the
           version of Falcon you're running.  If you're running 3.05,

           we set the HIDDEN bit to make Falcon use our squad6.mis file;
           if you're running 3.04, we set the READ-ONLY bit to make Falcon
           use our squad6.mis file.

           Although Falcon won't overwrite the SQUAD6.MIS file on the
           CALLER's machine (because StratFalc sets that read- only/hidden
           bit directly), it does overwrite it on the answerers'. We need
           a method of getting that modified, read- only/hidden SQUAD6.MIS
           to all the machines on the network. FTP?  Not bloody likely--
           it's Sneakernet!  Yes, it's true: we hand-carry the SQUAD6.MIS
           file from machine to machine, and we have to do this *between





           Strategic FALCON        Version 1.2 Update          Page 15
           ------------------------------------------------------------

           every mission*!  HEY, DON'T YELL AT ME!  I *TOLD* YOU THIS WAS
           SQUIRRELY!

           Calmed down yet?  I've supplied a few batch files to make this
           easier for all involved.

           First, you need a formatted disk with some free space on it.
           Label it "Secret Mission:  Eyes Only."  (Label it anything you
           like!)

           To facilitate matters, we're going to set an environment
           variable, FALCONDIR.

           III.  On all machines, type "SET FALCONDIR=C:\FALCON3"; press
           <Enter>. (Modify this to suit the path of the specific
           machine.)  It's a good idea to place this in the system's
           autoexec.bat for future sessions.

           Also, on the CALLER's machine, the StratFalc directory has to
           be on the SAME drive as the FALCON directory.

           IV.  On the CALLER's machine, in the \STRAT directory, copy
           either COMMS304.BAT or COMMS305.BAT to USER.BAT.  Copy the one
           that reflects the version of Falcon you're using.  (If you have
           a USER.BAT already, rename it before doing this.)

           V. If the desired floppy drive for the Sneakernet disk on the
           CALLER's machine is NOT drive a:, you'll have to modify
           USER.BAT accordingly.

           VI. On all machines, delete SQUAD6.*.

           VII. Have the caller start a new campaign using SQUAD0.  (If
           you are not using SQUAD0, you'll again have to modify USER.BAT
           to reflect the squad you ARE using.)  The caller should go
           through the mission screens and get into the cockpit.  Don't
           bother changing anything here;  you'll tweak the mission in
           StratFalc.  Exit from Falcon.

           VIII. Start Strategic Falcon and load the mission.  Modify it
           however you like, including changing the CALLER's plane. When
           satisfied, the caller MUST USE <F6> to exit StratFalc,
           answering "Yes" to "Save Changes?".

           IX.  If everything is working correctly, you'll be prompted to
           insert the Sneakernet disk.  Do so.

           X. After the file is copied, bring the disk to each machine in
           the net, put it into the floppy drive, and *log onto the
           drive*. Type "FALCOM"; press <Enter>.





           Strategic FALCON        Version 1.2 Update          Page 16
           ------------------------------------------------------------

           XI.  The caller should select the squad0 squadron in his main
           screen and enter comms.

           XII.  When all machines have been FALCOMed, you're all set.
           Everyone start your engines.

           XIII.  After the mission, the caller should go through the
           screens and get into the cockpit again.  He should then exit
           Falcon;  *after* he's out, the other machines should exit
           Falcon.  The caller will automatically be placed back in
           StratFalc to modify the new mission (USER.BAT takes care of the
           squad file number manipulation).  By using <F6> to exit, the
           cycle starts again.

           By the way, figure out yet why this won't work over the phone
           line?  It's a long walk to the answerer's machine! But this is
           really the only holdup to modem use of comms--if you want to e-
           mail the file to the answerer's machine, or establish a library
           of missions to fly together and have them on both machines,
           that should work. Just make sure to set the hidden or read-only
           byte on SQUAD6.MIS on both machines before starting comms (For
           Falcon 3.05, "ATTRIB +H SQUAD6.MIS"; for 3.04, "ATTRIB +R
           SQUAD6.MIS").

           In case it isn't obvious, this is a very raw outline of the way
           comms should be run.  It's tedious and, er, inelegant--and
           that's being kind.  If you and your group come up with any
           improvements, please let me know!

           Here are the instructions once more in an easier to read and
           print format:

           I.      If you're using Falcon 3.05, run 305FIX.EXE on all
                   machines on the network (or both machines on the null-
                   modem cable).  You only have to do this once.

           II.     Run COMMHACK ALTER on all machines on the network (or
                   both machines on the null-modem cable). You only have
                   to do this once.

           III.    On all machines, type "SET FALCONDIR=C:\FALCON3";
                   press <Enter>. (Modify this to suit the path of the
                   specific machine.)  It's a good idea to place this in
                   your autoexec.bat for future sessions. You only have
                   to do this once.

           IV.     On the CALLER's machine, in the \STRAT directory, copy
                   COMMS304.BAT or COMMS305.BAT to USER.BAT.  You only
                   have to do this once.





           Strategic FALCON        Version 1.2 Update          Page 17
           ------------------------------------------------------------

           V.      If the floppy drive for the Sneakernet disk on the
                   CALLER's machine is NOT drive a:, you'll have to
                   modify USER.BAT accordingly. You only have to do this
                   once.

           VI.     On all machines, delete SQUAD6.*. You should only have
                   to do this once per session, but if problems occur you
                   may have to do it between missions.

           VII.    Have the caller start a new campaign using SQUAD0. (If
                   you are not using SQUAD0, you'll again have to modify
                   USER.BAT to reflect the squad you ARE using.) You only
                   have to do this once per session.

           VIII.   The caller should go through the mission screens and
                   get into the cockpit.  Don't bother changing anything
                   here;  you'll tweak the mission in StratFalc.  Exit
                   from Falcon.

           IX.     Start Strategic Falcon and load the mission.  Modify
                   it however you like, including changing the CALLER's
                   plane.  When satisfied, the caller MUST USE <F6> to
                   exit StratFalc, answering "Yes" to "Save Changes?".

           X.      If everything is working correctly, you'll be prompted
                   to insert the Sneakernet disk.  Do so.

           XI.     After the file is copied, bring the disk to each
                   machine in the net, put it into the floppy drive, and
                   *log onto the drive*. Type "FALCOM"; press <Enter>.

           XII.    When all machines have been FALCOMed, you're all set.

           XIII.   The CALLER should select the proper squadron in his
                   main screen and enter comms.  The answerers should
                   start as they normally would.

           XIV.    After the mission, the CALLER should go through the
                   screens and get into the cockpit again.  He should
                   then exit Falcon;  *after* he's out, the other
                   machines should exit Falcon.  The CALLER will
                   automatically be placed back in StratFalc to modify
                   the new mission (USER.BAT takes care of the squad file
                   number manipulation).  By using <F6> to exit, the
                   cycle starts again.  You're back at step IX; repeat as
                   desired.


